  "query_preview": "..Since midnight, rain gauge and radar data suggest a\nwidespread area 2 to 4 inches of rain have fallen across and \nsouth of the Mogollon Rim, with some stream gauges exceeding\nrecord stage heights. There have been several reports of \nflooding/flash flooding, especially along and south of the Rim. \nRain has been spreading northward across the Navajo Nation and the\nKaibab Plateau, and while rainfall totals are expected to be \nlower north of the Mogollon Rim, flooding/flash flooding is still \npossible through this afternoon. As a mid-level shortwave trough\nmoves northeast into the Southwest, latest hi-res models and \nmesoanalysis suggest a transition from widespread rain, to more \nscattered showers and isolated thunderstorms pattern this \nafternoon by 3 to 4 pm, allowing for some breaks in rain, but \nkeeping the risk for periods of heavy rainfall across northern \nArizona through is afternoon and early evening hours. The risk for\nstrong or severe thunderstorms is low due to limited instability \nand moisture helping to prevent low and mid-level lapse rates from\nsteeping. However, cannot rule out some strong gusts near \nthunderstorms. After 8pm MST, shower and thunderstorm activity \nshould be on the decrease as the mid-level trough starts to push \noff to the east and drier air follows in the wake of a frontal \npassage. The Flood Watch remains in effect through late tonight as\nwater will continue to be draining through the washes, stream, \nand river basins, keeping waters levels high. River flooding may \ncontinue to be an issue through Monday and at least Tuesday.\n\nA Wind Advisory remains in effect through 11 PM MST for the Little\nColorado River Valley, Eastern Mogollon Rim, and White Mountains\nwere gusts as high as 40-50 mph are possible. Peak wind gusts will\ndecrease overnight, but gusts around 30 mph will be possible with\nthe frontal passage after 11 pm Monday to 8am Tuesday. There is \nalso potential for snow mostly above 7500-8000 ft elevation before\nthings fully dry out on Sunday night. The main area of concern is\nthe Kaibab Plateau where 1-2 inches of snow are possible. A \ndusting of snow will also be possible for the higher elevation \nlocations around the Flagstaff area. Fully dry conditions are \nexpected by Monday afternoon and through the week as ridging \ndominates. Another trough will brush past Arizona on Wednesday, \nbut this looks to remain mostly dry at this time\n\nThere is also potential for snow mostly above 7200-7800 ft \nelevation before things fully dry out tonight. The main area of \nconcern is the Kaibab Plateau where 1-2 inches of snow are \npossible. A dusting of snow will also be possible for the higher \nelevation locations around the Flagstaff area, with potentially\naround 1 inch near the top of the San Francisco Peaks. Fully dry \nconditions are expected by late Monday morning and through the \nweek as ridging dominates. Another trough will brush past Arizona \naround Wednesday or early Thursday, but this looks to remain \nmostly dry at this time.",
